      "text": "To solve the equation $7^{x+7} = 8^x$ for $x$, we can take the natural logarithm (ln) of both sides:\n\n$\\ln(7^{x+7}) = \\ln(8^x)$\n\nUsing the logarithm property $\\ln(a^b) = b\\ln(a)$, we get:\n\n$(x+7)\\ln(7) = x\\ln(8)$\n\nExpanding the equation, we get:\n\n$7x\\ln(7) + 7\\ln(7) = 8x\\ln(8)$\n\nIsolating $x$ by subtracting $8x\\ln(8)$ from both sides, we get:\n\n$7x\\ln(7) - 8x\\ln(8) = -7\\ln(7)$\n\nFactoring out $x$ on the left side, we get:\n\n$x(7\\ln(7) - 8\\ln(8)) = -7\\ln(7)$\n\nFinally, divide both sides by $(7\\ln(7) - 8\\ln(8))$ to solve for $x$:\n\n$x = \\frac{-7\\ln(7)}{7\\ln(7) - 8\\ln(8)}$\n\nRecall that $x = \\log_b 7^7$, so we can write the equation:\n\n$\\log_b 7^7 = \\frac{-7\\ln(7)}{7\\ln(7) - 8\\ln(8)}$\n\nTo solve for $b$, we can convert the logarithmic equation to its exponential form:\n\n$b^{\\log_b 7^7} = 7^7$\n\nUsing the property of logarithms $\\log_b(b^a) = a$, we get:\n\n$7^7 = 7^7$\n\nTherefore, $b = \\boxed{7}$.The answer is: 7",
