      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\(7^{x+7} = 8^x\\) and express the solution in the form \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Rewrite the equation using properties of exponents:**\n   \\[\n   7^{x+7} = 8^x\n   \\]\n   We can split the left-hand side using the exponent rule \\(a^{m+n} = a^m \\cdot a^n\\):\n   \\[\n   7^x \\cdot 7^7 = 8^x\n   \\]\n\n2. **Express both sides with the same base:**\n   \\[\n   7^x \\cdot 7^7 = (7 \\cdot 8)^x\n   \\]\n   Simplify the right-hand side:\n   \\[\n   7^x \\cdot 7^7 = 56^x\n   \\]\n\n3. **Rewrite the equation with a common base:**\n   \\[\n   (7 \\cdot 56)^x = 56^x\n   \\]\n   Simplify the left-hand side:\n   \\[\n   392^x = 56^x\n   \\]\n\n4. **Separate the exponents:**\n   \\[\n   392^x / 56^x = 1\n   \\]\n   Simplify the ratio:\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\frac{392}{56}\\right)^x = 1\n   \\]\n   Simplify the fraction:\n   \\[\n   6.5^x = 1\n   \\]\n\n5. **Express the base as a logarithm:**\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\frac{56}{392}\\right)^x = \\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)^x = 1\n   \\]\n   This implies:\n   \\[\n   \\log_{\\frac{1}{7}} 1 = x\n   \\]\n\n6. **Solve for \\(x\\):**\n   \\[\n   x = \\log_{\\frac{1}{7}} 1\n   \\]\n\n7. **Express \\(x\\) in the form \\(\\log_b 7^7\\):**\n   \\[\n   x = \\log_{\\frac{1}{7}} 1 = \\log_{a^k b^m} n = \\frac{\\log_a n}{k m}\n   \\]\n   Here, \\(a = \\frac{1}{7}\\), \\(b = 7\\), \\(m = 1\\), \\(k = 1\\), and \\(n = 1\\). Thus:\n   \\[\n   x = \\log_{\\frac{1}{7}} 1 = \\frac{\\log_{\\frac{1}{7}} 1}{1 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{\\log_{\\frac{1}{7}} 1}{1^1} = \\log_{\\frac{1}{7}} 1 = \\log_{\\frac{1}{7}} 1 = \\log_{\\frac{1}{7}} 1 = \\log_{\\frac{1}{7}} 1\n   \\]\n\n8. **Thus, \\(b = \\frac{1}{7}\\).**\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{7}}\n\\]",
